WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.620
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:10.499
Today we are going to immerse ourselves into
a wonderful art of rigid body disentanglement,

00:00:10.499 --> 00:00:13.990
or in simpler words, we are going to solve
these puzzles!

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:19.150
To be more exact, we’ll sit and enjoy our
time while a learning-based algorithm is going

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:22.640
to miraculously solve some really challenging
puzzles.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.720
Yes, this is going to be a spicy paper.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:30.289
Now, these puzzles are roughly ordered by
difficulty, where the easier ones are on the

00:00:30.289 --> 00:00:34.630
left, and as we traverse to the right, things
get more and more difficult.

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:39.680
And get this, this new technique is able to
algorithmically solve all of them.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.650
If you are like me and you don’t believe
a word of what’s been said, let’s see

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:48.550
if it lives up to its promise by solving three
of them, in increasing difficulty.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:51.000
Let’s start with an easy one.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.440
Example number one.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.300
Here, the algorithm recognizes that we need
to pull the circular part of the red piece

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:02.060
through the blue one and apply the appropriate
rotations to make sure that we don’t get

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:04.019
stuck during this process.

00:01:04.019 --> 00:01:08.050
While we finish this sequence, please note
that this video contains spoilers for some

00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.440
well-known puzzles.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:15.630
If you wish to experience them yourself, pause
this video, and, I guess, buy and try them.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:20.190
This one was good enough to warm ourselves
up, so let’s hop on to the next one.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.400
Example number two, the duet puzzle.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:28.800
Well that was quite a bit of a jump in difficulty
because we seem stuck right at the start.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:35.390
Hmm… this seems flat out impossible…until
the algorithm recognizes that there are these

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:40.700
small notches in the puzzle, and if we rotate
the red piece correctly, we may go from one

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.950
cell to the next one.

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:49.010
Great, so now it’s not impossible anymore,
it is more like a maze that we have to climb

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.010
through.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.520
But the challenges are still not over.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.010
Where is the endpoint?

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:55.729
How do we finish this puzzle?

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:02.010
There has to be a notch on the side…yes,
there is this one or this one, so we ultimately

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:12.689
have to end up in one of these places…and,
there we go.

00:02:12.689 --> 00:02:13.810
Bravo!

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:15.540
Experiment number three.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.939
My favorite, the enigma.

00:02:17.939 --> 00:02:21.950
Hmm..this is exactly the opposite of the previous
one.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.709
It looks so easy!

00:02:23.709 --> 00:02:29.749
Just get the tiny opening of the red piece
onto this part of the blue one, and we are

00:02:29.749 --> 00:02:31.239
done.

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:32.730
Uh-oh.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.310
The curved part is in the way.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:40.480
Something that seemed so easy now suddenly
seems absolutely impossible!

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.239
But fortunately, this learning algorithm does
not get discouraged, and does not know what

00:02:45.239 --> 00:02:51.310
impossible is, and it finds this tricky series
of rotations to go around the entirety of

00:02:51.310 --> 00:02:54.939
the blue piece, and then, finish the puzzle.

00:02:54.939 --> 00:02:55.989
Glorious.

00:02:55.989 --> 00:02:56.989
What a rollercoaster!

00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:00.819
A hallmark of an expertly designed puzzle.

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:05.340
And to experience more of these puzzles, make
sure to have a look paper and its website

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.819
with a super fun interactive app.

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:12.260
If you do, you will also learn really cool
new things…for instance, if you get back

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.919
home in the middle of the night after a long
day, and two of your keys are stuck together,

00:03:17.919 --> 00:03:20.530
you will know exactly how to handle it.

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:25.310
And all this can be done through the power
of machine learning and computer graphics

00:03:25.310 --> 00:03:26.459
research.

00:03:26.459 --> 00:03:28.349
What a time to be alive!

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:31.090
So how does this wizardry work exactly?

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.830
The key techniques here are tunnel discovery
and path planning.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:40.319
First, a neural network looks at the puzzle
and identifies where the gaps and notches

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:44.949
are, and specifies the starting position and
the goal position that we need to achieve

00:03:44.949 --> 00:03:46.299
to finish the puzzle.

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:52.260
Then, a set of collision-free key configurations
are identified, after which, the blooming

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.329
step can commence.

00:03:54.329 --> 00:03:56.040
So what does that do?

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.939
Well, the goal is to be able to go through
these narrow tunnels that represent tricky

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:06.010
steps in the puzzles that typically require
some unintuitive rotations.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:10.829
These are typically the most challenging parts
of the puzzles, and the blooming step starts

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:15.439
from these narrow tunnels and helps us reach
the bigger bubbles of the puzzle.

00:04:15.439 --> 00:04:20.319
But as you see, not all roads connect, or
at least, not easily.

00:04:20.319 --> 00:04:25.900
The forest connect step tries to connect these
roads through collision-free paths, and now,

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.680
finally, all we have to do is find the shortest
path from the start to the endpoint to solve

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:32.840
the puzzle.

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:38.360
And also, according to my internal numbering
system, this is Two Minute Papers episode

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:40.690
number 478.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:45.110
And to every single one of you Fellow Scholars
who are watching this, thank you so much to

00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:49.460
all of you for being with us for so long on
this incredible journey.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:55.540
Man, I love my job, and I jump out of bed
full of energy and joy knowing that I get

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.770
to read research papers and flip out together
with many of you Fellow Scholars on a regular

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:01.840
basis.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:06:10.410
Thank you.

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:13.001
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

